        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          To provide a suitable protective environment during both lunar surface and free space excursions, the astronaut carried a compact assembly on his back.  This assembly, which was comprised of various environmental control devices, was collectively called the Portable Life Support System (PLSS).  The PLSS supplied breathing oxygen and recycled oxygen by removing carbon dioxide, odors, moisture, and some trace contaminant gases.  Suit temperature and pressure control was provided as well as voice communications and telemetry data.  The PLSS provided warnings of system malfunctions.  It supplied oxygen to the pressure garment assembly (PGA), or space suit, and cooling water to the liquid cooling garment (LCG), which was worn under the space suit.  
<P>
The PLSS has several sub-systems: an oxygen ventilating circuit, a primary oxygen subsystem, a liquid transport loop, a feedwater loop, and an electrical power sub-system.  The PLSS was designed for a 4-hour lunar exploration mission and an average metabolic rate of 1200 Btu/hr.  The requirement for two extravehicular activity (EVA) periods dictated that the PLSS could be recharged with consumables (oxygen, water, lithium hydroxide, and battery) in the lunar module between EVAs.
<P>
Much of the PLSS instrumentation was located in the remote control unit.  This chest-mounted unit housed electrical controls for the PLSS, a primary oxygen quantity indicator, and warning devices.  The warning devices would signal the astronaut if system components failed to work properly.  Malfunctions checked were low feedwater pressure, low ventilation flow, low PGA pressure, and high oxygen flow.  
<P>
Oxygen Ventilating Circuit
<P>
The oxygen ventilating circuit provided temperature, humidity, and contaminant control of breathing oxygen.  Recycled gas and fresh oxygen entered the suit, adsorbing heat, moisture, and body contaminants.  The contaminated gas returned to the PLSS contaminant control assembly where contaminants were removed.  The decontaminated gas then entered the sublimator (heat exchanger) where heat was given up, and the excess moisture in the stream was condensed.  Next, water was removed by a water separator and transferred to a storage reservoir.  A fan forced the air through a back flow check valve, finishing the recycling process.
<P>
Primary Oxygen Subsystem
<P>
The gaseous oxygen in the PLSS primary oxygen subsystem provided oxygen for suit pressurization and astronaut breathing.  Oxygen, stored in the primary bottle, was regulated to the correct pressure before entering the rest of the system.  A quick-fill connector allowed for oxygen recharging.
<P>
Liquid Transport Loop
<P>
The liquid transport loop was the primary means of  crewman temperature control.  Water from the liquid cooling garment entered the loop through the multiple water connector.  The water was then pumped through the sublimator where heat was removed.  The cooled water was used to cool the fan motor before returning to the LCG.
<P>
Feedwater Loop
<P>
The feedwater loop supplied expendable water to the sublimator for cooling and storing condensation removed by the water separator in the oxygen ventilation circuit.  As the water passed through the sublimator, it absorbed system heat.  The hot water was then discharged to the outside.
<P>
Electrical Power Subsystem
<P>
The electrical power subsystem provided electrical power to the fan and pump motor assemblies, the communications systems and the instrumentation.  The extravehicular communications system (EVCS) provided voice communications and telemetry transmission of system operation.<END>
